# URL Components

The **URL**, the location you type into a browser's address bar, has several components, and understanding them matters as we start deploying pages publicly. Here is one with every part present:

```text
https://blueserver.com:8000/path?type=example#section2
```

## The Protocol

`https` is the **protocol**: the language the browser uses to talk to whatever is serving the content. It is followed by `://`.

Browsing the web, the protocol is almost always `http` (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) or `https`, a secure version that encrypts the data sent and received. During development you may also see `file`, which means the browser is reading from your own computer's file system rather than from a server at all.

## The Host Name

`blueserver.com` is the **host name**: the name of the computer or server we are talking to. There are a great many servers in the world, and the host name says which one to fetch from.

When you run a server on your own machine, the host name is usually `localhost`, which is a name for your own computer.

## The Path

After the host name comes a `/` and then, optionally, the **path**: the location of what we are asking for on that server. The server has a file system somewhere, perhaps with a `home` directory and a `path` directory, and the path says which part of it we want.

If the path points at a directory rather than a file, the browser looks for a file named `index.html` there by default. That is why `https://blueserver.com/` and `https://blueserver.com/index.html` usually show the same page.

Those three parts together are the shape of most of the URLs you meet:

![The URL https://blueserver.com/path, with a bracket under each part and a label beneath it: https is labeled Protocol, blueserver.com is labeled Host Name, and path is labeled Path. The separators, the colon slash slash and the slash, are drawn in gray and left unlabeled.](images/protocol-host-path.png)

## The Query

After a `?` comes the **query**, which gives the server more specifics about what we are asking for. The server reads the query and decides exactly what content to send back.

The University of Michigan events site is a good example. Visiting the page for this month gives a path like `/month`, and then filtering by event type adds a query such as `?type=7`. What `7` means is internal to that site: it is a message from the page to its own server, and the server decides what to do with it.

## The Port

`:8000` after the host name is the **port**. If the server is a house, the port is which door to knock on.

You rarely write one, because each protocol has a default that is assumed when you leave it out:

- `http` defaults to port **80**.
- `https` defaults to port **443**.

Development tools, though, open servers on all sorts of ports, so locally you write the port explicitly. Common ones are `3000`, `8000`, `8080`, and `8888`. An address like `http://localhost:3000/` says "the server running on my own computer, listening on door 3000".

The port goes after the host name, separated by a colon, which gives the other shape you will see constantly while developing:

![The URL http://localhost:8000/path, with a bracket under each part and a label beneath it: http is labeled Protocol, localhost is labeled Host Name, 8000 is labeled Port, and path is labeled Path.](images/protocol-host-port-path.png)

## The Fragment

Finally, after a `#`, comes the **fragment**, which says which part of the document the browser should show. The browser typically uses it to scroll to a particular section.

You will not see all of these in every URL. Often it is just a protocol, a host name, and maybe a path.

## Developing Locally

While building a page on your own machine, you will see two shapes of address:

- **`localhost` with a port**, such as `http://localhost:3000/`. There is a real server running, and it behaves like any other server; it is just that only your computer can reach it.
- **The `file` protocol**, such as `file:///Users/you/project/index.html`. No server is involved at all; the browser is reading the file straight off your disk. Open the parent directory this way and most browsers show a file navigator you can click through.

Deploying, which is the subject of the next two lessons, means moving the page off `localhost` and onto a server anyone can reach.

## Reading the Parts From JavaScript

We met `window.location` back when we looked at [the `window` object](<04 - Built-in Objects window and document.webnb#the-window-object>). It is not just a string: it is an object with a property for each of the components above.

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(window.location.href);
console.log(window.location.protocol);
console.log(window.location.hostname);
console.log(window.location.port);
console.log(window.location.pathname);
console.log(window.location.search);
console.log(window.location.hash);
```

Run that and compare the values with the address this notebook is loaded from. A few details are worth knowing:

- `href` is the whole URL as one string.
- `protocol` includes the trailing colon, so it reads `https:` rather than `https`.
- `port` is an empty string when the URL relies on the default, and something like `3000` when a port is written out.
- `pathname` is only the path, with no query or fragment.
- `search` is the query *including* the leading `?`, and `hash` is the fragment including the `#`.

### Parsing Any URL

JavaScript also gives us a `URL` object that breaks apart any URL string, not only the page we happen to be on:

```{javascript autorun}
const url = new URL('https://blueserver.com:8000/path?type=example#section2');

console.log(url.protocol);
console.log(url.hostname);
console.log(url.port);
console.log(url.pathname);
console.log(url.search);
console.log(url.hash);
```

The `new` keyword there is how JavaScript builds certain kinds of object; you will see much more of it later in the specialization. For now, treat `new URL(someString)` as "give me an object with all the pieces of this URL", and read each logged value against the string above it.

## Exercises

1. Read three parts of the current page's address into variables: the protocol, the host name, and the path.

    ```{javascript}
    const currentProtocol = /* Your code here */;
    const currentHost = /* Your code here */;
    const currentPath = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(currentProtocol, currentHost, currentPath);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const currentProtocol = window.location.protocol;
    const currentHost = window.location.hostname;
    const currentPath = window.location.pathname;

    console.log(currentProtocol, currentHost, currentPath);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(currentProtocol).should('equal', window.location.protocol).run('`currentProtocol` should be `window.location.protocol`', 'Read the protocol');
    wrap(currentHost).should('equal', window.location.hostname).run('`currentHost` should be `window.location.hostname`', 'Read the host name');
    wrap(currentPath).should('equal', window.location.pathname).run('`currentPath` should be `window.location.pathname`', 'Read the path');
    ```

2. Complete `describeURL` so that it returns an object holding the `hostname`, `port`, and `pathname` of whatever URL string it is given.

    ```{javascript}
    function describeURL(urlString) {
      const url = new URL(urlString);

      return {
        hostname: /* the host name */,
        port: /* the port */,
        pathname: /* the path */
      };
    }

    console.log(describeURL('http://localhost:3000/dashboard/index.html'));
    ```
    ```+solution
    function describeURL(urlString) {
      const url = new URL(urlString);

      return {
        hostname: url.hostname,
        port: url.port,
        pathname: url.pathname
      };
    }

    console.log(describeURL('http://localhost:3000/dashboard/index.html'));
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(describeURL('http://localhost:3000/dashboard/index.html').hostname).should('equal', 'localhost').run('`hostname` should be `localhost`', 'Read the host name');
    wrap(describeURL('http://localhost:3000/dashboard/index.html').port).should('equal', '3000').run('`port` should be `3000`', 'Read the port');
    wrap(describeURL('http://localhost:3000/dashboard/index.html').pathname).should('equal', '/dashboard/index.html').run('`pathname` should be `/dashboard/index.html`', 'Read the path');
    wrap(describeURL('https://umich.edu/about').port).should('equal', '').run('`port` should be an empty string when the URL leaves the port out', 'Handled the default port');
    ```

3. A URL is served over `https` and does not write out a port. Which port is used?

    ```{mcq}
    `https://umich.edu/about` has no port in it. Which port does the browser connect to?
    correct: Correct! `https` defaults to port 443, and `http` defaults to port 80, which is why those two are almost never written out.
    wrong: Look back at "The Port" and the two protocol defaults listed there.
    [x] 443
    [ ] 80
    [ ] 3000
    [ ] None; a URL without a port uses no port at all
    ```

